  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday's numbers surface a critical operational issue masked by top-line gains: both locations are severely under-capacity heading into June, and the data tells a story of transition rather than strength.\n\nPort Washington's 69% revenue jump is entirely retail-driven (up 250%) while grooming flatlined\u2014a sign that yesterday you had bathers working (4 bather-hours) but zero groomers, so the team was retailing to walk-ins rather than servicing booked appointments. The add-on attach rate collapsed from 50% to 25%, confirming no grooming happened. This is a scheduling anomaly, not performance. Hicksville moved one groomer yesterday (Julia B) and she produced $180\u2014well below the trailing-30-day baseline of $384/groomer/day\u2014another signal of reduced throughput. The real concern: both locations are showing zero appointments month-to-date against DOW-adjusted expectations of $2,547 (Port) and $579 (Hicksville). This is not calendar noise; this is a capacity cliff. June is starting behind the eight-ball.\n\nThe bright spot: year-to-date through May, Port Washington is up 53% in revenue and 55% in appointments versus last year\u2014that's genuine business building. But Hicksville's visit-cadence drift is ticking up (12+ week gap customers growing 1.8 percentage points), a leading indicator of stealth churn you should watch closely. You also have a $310k lapse pool at Port that's ripe for reactivation if capacity allows.\n\nReorder X-Small Treat Tables (out of stock at both locations, negative inventory at Hicksville) and Hypoallergenic Shampoo immediately\u2014these are operational blockers.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you have 4 groomer-days across both locations with 7 bather hours at Port; tomorrow 8 groomer-days with zero bather coverage; next seven days total 40 groomer-days and 43 bather hours\u2014schedule is lean for a June ramp."
